  "evaluated_on": "2026-10-15",
  "hypothesis": "weakening",
  "design": "The framework started in January 2012 everywhere except WA (August 2012). The only clean comparison is 2011-12, when the other states had it and WA did not: a first-year effect. Method: stacked difference-in-differences, net of the same comparison for unaffected industries in the same states.",
  "comparison": "Treated: ACT, NSW, NT, QLD, SA, TAS, VIC. Comparison: WA.",
  "window": "2007-08 to 2011-12 (first exposed year 2011-12)",
  "verdict": "inconclusive",
  "finding_label": "No detectable effect",
  "grade": "Moderate",
  "grade_reason": "Passes the plan's pre-reform check and robustness variants; one or few treated states, so only large effects are detectable.",
  "headline": "In 2011-12, the framework's first year, childcare business entry in the states that had it changed by −0.3 per 100 relative to WA, which started seven months later (90% interval −5.9 to +4.3); exits by +3.1 (90% interval −1.4 to +6.9). Neither is distinguishable from zero.",
  "category_summaries": {
    "competition": "No detectable first-year effect on entry or exit. With one comparison state, only effects above about 10 per 100 could have been detected.",
    "prices": "Descriptive only. Childcare fees move with subsidies, and only two quarters separate the states.",
    "productivity": "Not measurable. Childcare is a small part of health care and social assistance. Shown for description only."
  },
